From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mihawk <163614687+mihawk-99@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 10:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: a thread's guard follows a stack that ran past it.

Patch 1120 put a new thread's guard page below its committed top, as Windows does, and left the pages below committed.
Code that runs past the guard without touching it then lands below StackLimit with no fault: ntdll's __chkstk does not
probe, and a frame larger than a page skips it (kernel32 heap's test_GlobalAlloc has 512 KiB of locals). An exception
there could not be dispatched: every handler frame was below the limit ("Exception frame is not in stack limits") and
the process ended, kernel32 heap in both builds, where the pinned Linux Wine (its whole stack committed, its guard at the
bottom) catches the access violation GlobalSize(0xdeadbee0) raises (2026-10-26). When an exception's frame lies below
StackLimit on a committed page that is not a guard, the old guard is cleared and grow_thread_stack moves the guard and
the limit down to it, as a probe would have; inside the guaranteed space it still raises STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW.
---
diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
index 5a5cf36..b9b0a31 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -6435,8 +6435,25 @@ void *virtual_setup_exception( void *stack_ptr, size_t size, EXCEPTION_RECORD *r
     else if (stack < stack_info.limit)
     {
         char *page = ROUND_ADDR( stack, host_page_mask );
+        BYTE vprot;
         mutex_lock( &virtual_mutex );  /* no need for signal masking inside signal handler */
-        if ((get_host_page_vprot( page ) & VPROT_GUARD) && grow_thread_stack( page, &stack_info ))
+        vprot = get_host_page_vprot( page );
+        if (!(vprot & VPROT_GUARD) && (vprot & VPROT_COMMITTED))
+        {
+            /* The pages below a thread's guard stay committed (patch 1120), so code that runs past the guard without
+             * touching it lands below StackLimit with no fault: ntdll's __chkstk does not probe, and kernel32 heap's
+             * test_GlobalAlloc has 512 KiB of locals. The dispatcher then refused every handler frame there ("not in
+             * stack limits") and the process ended (kernel32 heap, both builds, 2026-10-26). The guard follows the stack
+             * down here, as a probe would have moved it. */
+            char *guard = (char *)stack_info.limit - host_page_size;
+            if (guard > page && (get_host_page_vprot( guard ) & VPROT_GUARD))
+            {
+                set_page_vprot_bits( guard, host_page_size, 0, VPROT_GUARD );
+                mprotect_range( guard, host_page_size, 0, 0 );
+            }
+            vprot |= VPROT_GUARD;
+        }
+        if ((vprot & VPROT_GUARD) && grow_thread_stack( page, &stack_info ))
         {
             rec->ExceptionCode = STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW;
             rec->NumberParameters = 0;
